- [Nevus Standard]: Nevi, also known as melanocytic nevi or skin moles, are commonly solitary, well-circumscribed pigmented skin lesions. Their size ranges from approximately 5-6 mm to 1.5 cm in diameter, showcasing key diagnostic features such as a homogeneous, uniform brown to dark brown pigmentation with varying degrees of gradient, from lighter tan to darker brown, accompanied by focal areas of concentrated pigmentation. Irregular borders, scalloped and notched margins, and a gradual transition into surrounding normal skin characterize their shape. The surfaces are smooth and non-scaly, with no evidence of crusting, ulceration, atrophy, lichenification, or telangiectasia. Internally, nevi display a consistently diffused pigment network with evenly dispersed dark brown to black globules and varying degrees of pigment homogeneity. Asymmetric structures and lack of radial symmetry are common. While some nevi may exhibit signs of regression, such as hypopigmentation or whitening, they generally remain stable and do not display active inflammation or malignancy. Monitoring these lesions for any changes in size, shape, or color over time is essential.
- [Scar Standard]: Scars can exhibit a range of attributes influenced by their underlying causes, encompassing inflammatory and reactive processes to malignancies and dermatosis conditions. Examination of scars should focus on well-defined borders, stable surface quality, and the absence of ulcers, crusts, atrophy, or lichenification. Irregular shapes, varying degrees of redness, pigmentation, vascular changes, and structural patterns may also be observed. Internal architectural patterns like pigment networks, globules, or regression structures are typically absent. When analyzing scars using dermoscopy, it is crucial to consider these core visual features for accurate diagnoses and differentiation among various scar types.
