[Handbook: Solar lentigo]
•    Solar lentigines: persistent flat, brown spots (liver spots) on exposed skin, e.g. backs of hands, forearms, face. Easy bruising (senile purpura) which resolves, leaving white stellate pseudoscars—​most obvious on forearms and backs of hands. Elastic tissue damaged by chronic sun exposure provides the small cutaneous blood vessels with inadequate support. Waxy coarse, yellowish skin (solar elastosis) on the forehead, temples, cheeks, and back of the neck, associated with deep furrows. •    Solar lentigines: persistent flat, brown spots (liver spots) on exposed skin, e.g. backs of hands, forearms, face. Easy bruising (senile purpura) which resolves, leaving white stellate pseudoscars—​most obvious on forearms and backs of hands. Elastic tissue damaged by chronic sun exposure provides the small cutaneous blood vessels with inadequate support. Waxy coarse, yellowish skin (solar elastosis) on the forehead, temples, cheeks, and back of the neck, associated with deep furrows.
