  Findings: This is a close-up dermoscopic image of a complex, asymmetric skin lesion exhibiting features suggestive of advanced malignant transformation. The lesion is primarily a nodular and ulcerated growth with a heterogeneous surface. It displays a mixture of colors including erythematous, brown, black, and blue-gray pigmentation, with the darker areas appearing as irregularly distributed, variably sized globules and clusters, some of which are coalescing. The borders are irregular, poorly defined, and appear to infiltrate the surrounding normal skin, lacking a clear demarcation. The surface is ulcerated, with areas of necrotic debris, crusting, and a translucent, waxy or keratinous overlay in some regions. There is evidence of central ulceration with surrounding erythema and a mixture of pigmented and non-pigmented zones. The lesion lacks symmetry and demonstrates a complex internal architecture with no discernible pigment network, but rather a chaotic arrangement of pigment, ulceration, and structural disruption. The presence of multiple dark globules, ulceration, and irregular borders are highly concerning for malignant melanoma or a similar aggressive neoplasm.


